{"causes":["The rise of the Franciscan order emphasized personal piety and emotional connection to the divine.","The 13th century saw a growing trend in devotional literature within the Catholic tradition.","The socio-political upheavals of the medieval period prompted a search for deeper spiritual expression among the laity."],"effects":["Stabat Mater inspired numerous musical compositions, notably Pergolesi's famous setting in 1736, which popularized the text in concert repertoire.","The poem has influenced artistic representations of the Virgin Mary and her suffering, shaping cultural perceptions of maternal grief.","Its use in liturgical practices continues to evoke contemplation of suffering and redemption in Christian communities worldwide."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"jacopone-da-todi","sourceName":"Jacopone da Todi","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"stabat-mater","targetName":"Stabat Mater","context":"Jacopone da Todi composed Stabat Mater around 1280, creating a profound expression of Marian devotion."},{"sourceSlug":"stabat-mater","sourceName":"Stabat Mater","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"giovanni-battista-pergolesi","targetName":"Giovanni Battista Pergolesi","context":"The emotional depth of Stabat Mater influenced Pergolesi's musical setting in 1736, which became a staple of Baroque sacred music."},{"sourceSlug":"stabat-mater","sourceName":"Stabat Mater","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"feast-of-our-lady-of-sorrows","targetName":"Feast of Our Lady of Sorrows","context":"Stabat Mater is integral to the liturgical celebrations of the Feast of Our Lady of Sorrows, highlighting its role in Catholic worship."},{"sourceSlug":"stabat-mater","sourceName":"Stabat Mater","verb":"TRANSMITS","targetSlug":"catholic-church","targetName":"Catholic Church","context":"Stabat Mater has been transmitted through generations as a significant text in Catholic liturgy and devotion."},{"sourceSlug":"stabat-mater","sourceName":"Stabat Mater","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"sacred-music","targetName":"Sacred Music","context":"Stabat Mater occurs within the tradition of sacred music, influencing the development of liturgical compositions."}],"places":[{"name":"Todi, Italy","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Rome, Italy","role":"Major liturgical center"},{"name":"Assisi, Italy","role":"Franciscan influence"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Stabat Mater","type":"Reference article","slug":"stabat-mater"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q210080","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stabat_Mater"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Stabat Mater contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Stabat Mater (Latin: 'The Mother Was Standing') is a medieval Catholic hymn attributed to the Italian Franciscan poet Jacopone da Todi (c. 1230–1306 CE), composed c. 1290–1300 CE and one of the most celebrated and widely set texts in the history of Western sacred music. The hymn consists of 20 stanzas in rhymed Latin verse describing the grief of the Virgin Mary at the foot of the cross during the crucifixion of Jesus — presenting a meditation on compassion (compassio), suffering, and the devotion of the believer who seeks to share in Mary's grief and in Christ's passion. The hymn begins: 'Stabat mater dolorosa / Iuxta crucem lacrimosa / Dum pendebat filius' ('The grieving mother was standing / Weeping beside the cross / While her Son was hanging there').

The Stabat Mater was widely used in Catholic liturgy and devotion — it was adopted as a sequence for the Feast of the Seven Sorrows of Our Lady and for the Stations of the Cross. Its text was set to music by virtually every major European composer from the 14th century to the 20th: Giovanni da Palestrina (c. 1590), Agostino Steffani, Alessandro Scarlatti, Vivaldi (1712), Pergolesi (1736 — the most performed setting, composed in the last weeks of his life), Haydn (1767), Boccherini, Schubert (1816), Rossini (1832/1841), Dvořák (1877), Verdi (1898), and Poulenc (1950/1951), among many others — making it the most multiply-composed Latin text other than the Mass and the Magnificat.

The Stabat Mater is a supreme expression of medieval Franciscan affective piety — the devotional tradition, pioneered by Francis of Assisi and his followers, that emphasised emotional identification with the suffering Christ and the grieving Mary as the primary method of spiritual growth. Its influence extended beyond Catholic devotion to Protestant musical culture (J.S. Bach's Widerstehe doch der Sünde is a parody of Pergolesi's Stabat Mater) and to the broader tradition of Western sacred art.","subjects":["Latin Literature","Medieval Era","Catholic Liturgy","Sacred Music","Franciscan Spirituality","Marian Devotion","Church Music","Italian Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Stabat Mater has shaped Christian liturgical practices and inspired generations of composers, resulting in a profound cultural and musical legacy that endures today.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
